requirements.text:
RoomKit provides a policy built on a classifier (Section 6.8). It asks narrow
questions about the turn, in one call, over the turn's text, the recent turns
named by speaker (the agent's own under its name) and the people, and composes
the answers in code, in this order:

1. silent when the speaker has not finished, postpones or declines asking, or
   asks the agent to keep quiet on this turn;
2. speak when the turn answers a question the agent just asked;
3. speak when the agent is asked directly or indirectly, or asked for
   something tentatively, or asked anything when one person talks with it;
4. offer when the speaker only wonders whether the agent could help;
5. silent otherwise.

A text cut short
  never leaves part of such a block open (it names the block instead, as a
  compaction does);

A classifier answers every question it was asked, or raises `ClassifierError`
for whatever kept it from answering (a refused request, an answer it cannot read,
the end of its wait, which it bounds).

- its own observation, of category `event_chain_depth_exceeded`, whose
  metadata holds `chain_depth` (the depth the response would have had),
  `max_chain_depth` and `source_channel` (the channel not asked);

Those registered
while no room held the id are held for the new room, fire for its creation,
and end with it in turn.

`needs_lock = false` is only meaningful on a SYNC `BEFORE_BROADCAST` hook;
implementations MUST reject it on any other trigger or execution mode at
registration.

- one that names its room, first thing at §10.1 step 1, once a call
    acting for an organization has had the room read with it (§17.2),
    nothing created;

A
bounded cleanup that expires MUST report incomplete drainage, not success;
resources still in use MUST be retained.

- its readers of state that lives in the owner's process (the room's
  background tasks, §23.4; what a vision provider last saw, §12.8.7), turned
  into owner calls;

The executor applies those changes to
the turn's record before the call returns:

- a key removed is removed;

**Cancellation.** The owner's ways of ending a turn early keep their local
meaning, each its own message:

- the reader stops reading: `close` (§10.6.4), a reader's close;

One that cannot be rebuilt so that it gives back its
  message (a field its message reads did not cross) comes back as the generic
  turn error below, also of that class, its message kept;

Both auditors use JSONL (JSON Lines) for persistence — one JSON object per
line, independently parseable:

```
{"ts":"...","agent_id":"exec","tool_name":"search","arguments":{"q":"roomkit"},"result":"...","status":"ok","duration_ms":1234.5,"metadata":{}}
{"ts":"...","type":"speech","role":"user","content":"Open Chrome","duration_ms":null,"metadata":{}}
```

Every recording
  configuration carries it (`encryption`, `storage_encrypted_at_rest`;
  Sections 12.3.7, 12.8.10, 12.10.8, 12.11).

A stage that must decode the audio (resampling, mixing,
  encoding for a transport) loses a file-level manifest by construction;
  implementations SHOULD document where that happens.

An Agent with a greeting and `auto_greet` greets on a session's start, through
a hook on ON_SESSION_STARTED of the kit, named `_agent_auto_greet:<agent>`, so
that the message that started the session waits for it (§10.1 step 2);

as does an agent whose handoff
is set up already, in every room or in one, an agent named twice, or one whose
tools hold the handoff's name;

An agent with neither a prompt nor an identity block to set
gives its session the channel's own prompt when a handoff makes it active, as
a session that starts with it gets;

While it holds, the router selects no agent and stamps
`_routed_to: _none_`, whichever rule or default would pick one, and the message
still reaches the room's transports;

A handoff to an agent MUST
be refused, by its tool and by the handler's own call alike, firing
`ON_HANDOFF_REJECTED`;

A handler whose event channel is a realtime channel the kit
holds MUST refuse an agent held by its declaration alone, or an intelligence
channel that is not an agent, when it is given its agents;

and the status bus (Section 19.8). The supervisor told the user
results would follow;
it is told they will not,

A sequential team is
supervised (the supervisor frames and validates each step) whether it runs
within the turn or in the background;

a worker whose
`delegate_to_<id>` holds only ASCII letters, digits, `_` and `-`, within 128
characters, keeps it;

A producer's task that failed (§23.3 step 6)
stops the loop: the last output it produced, if any, is the result, not
approved, and says the loop stopped on that failure;

a
turn its round cap, deadline, budget or a stop cut short is read there, is
no error and fires no ON_ERROR, as a room turn's does;

A
   turn backend that does not list its executors tells nothing, and the turn
   is given;

a person's message that names it gives it one
    turn, and it goes on listening (one that names nobody, routed to
    `everyone` or to the agents that asked that person, gives it none);

That last part is the host's `rules` when the discussion has
    them, `{handle}` in them standing for another agent's handle with its `@`
    (an example the agent can follow) and `{silent}` for the silence token;

an instruction addressed to an agent afterwards, and a regenerated
    answer asked for afterwards, are refused, their result blocked with the
    reason `discussion_over`;

Each turn's notes list
    the room's tasks (Section 23.4), so an agent asked how a task goes answers
    from them,

The arguments' check is a first gate,
not a validator: a name the schema's `required` lists that the call lacks
reads `missing required argument '<arg>'`;

Arguments
that do not fit the declared schema read `Invalid arguments for '<name>':
<why>`;

unless it
is that setup's own declaration (a realtime pipeline gives each agent's
session its own handoff and tools this way, Section 19.5);

The store's capacity is counted per room, and a bound the whole store
  needs for memory MUST take from the room holding the most first, so a room
  holding few results keeps them while other rooms evict;

Direct realtime injection has no atomic transaction with the remote service.
Suppressing retries after uncertainty can leave an announcement undelivered;
eliminating that tradeoff requires verified idempotency at the remote service.

`notify` names who is told: an
   intelligence channel receives it addressed to it, through the room's
   transport (§10.1.1);

The message names the task (`task`, quoted as
   Section 6.4 quotes a text from outside) before the result, so the room
   tells what it answers;

A turn that
ends without the call is re-prompted with the tool's reminder, a system
message only intelligence reads (step 4), up to `max_result_retries` times;

A `task` that is no text, or a `share_channels` that
  is no list, is refused, nothing made, and an `agent` that is no text names no
  channel of the kit (a model may send them whatever the tool declares);

the task,
  its status, its latest progress and when it was posted (Section 23.3), its
  whole result (Section 19.8) or that it failed, and when it was posted;

```
├── allowed_tools: string | list<string> | null  # Tool access patterns, null by default;
```

That turn's manifest, its activation,
reference and script tools and its skill gating MUST read it in place of the
channel's;

Registering a server
again replaces its entries: a skill it no longer offers, or offers in a form
the host does not take, retires and is served nowhere any more;

This replaces process-dependent set display
strings: a server publishing that former string MUST publish the canonical
array instead;

an entry it
returns is registered as a listed one would be, kept out of the manifest
(`mark_unlisted`), and activated;

Content kept on disk between reads MUST meet the
extension's cache requirements (out of reach of what the model runs, verified
again on each read, outside every directory a registry discovers skills in);

A transport failure is
retryable only when the request never left (the connection could not be
made);
